- How would you reconstruct the actual segment boundaries from prev?
- Could you optimize to O(nk) with advanced DP techniques or convex hull tricks?
- How would changes to c (per-segment cost) affect the optimal k if it’s not fixed?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How to adapt if dominoes have variable lengths or weights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a fundamental DP pattern with an explicit gap constraint, a natural generalization of the classic non-adjacent selection.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index and remaining picks
- Handling fixed gap via i-g-1 transition
- Space- and time-complexity considerations
- Reconstruction of chosen indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumWithGap(A, k, g){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i-1
      if(t>0 && i-g-1>=0 && dp[i-g-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-g-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = i-g-1; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], indices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the exact indices efficiently?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k picks or variable gap?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with partitioning and a gap constraint, requiring prefix sums, 2D DP, and backtracking to output block endpoints. Encourages thinking about edge cases when n is large and k near n/gap.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes with fixed block counts
- Enforcing gap constraints between blocks
- Backtracking to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKBlocks(profits, k, g) {
  const n = profits.length
  const S = Array(n + 1).fill(0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) S[i + 1] = S[i] + profits[i]
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity))
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1))
  dp[0][0] = 0
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t])
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i - 1 - g; l++) {
        const val = dp[l][t - 1] + (S[i] - S[l])
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l }
      }
    }
  }
  const blocks = []
  let i = n, t = k
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = take[i][t]
    blocks.unshift([l, i - 1])
    i = l
    t--
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a sophisticated dynamic programming variant where the non-adjacency constraint introduces complexity beyond standard interval selection problems. It requires careful state design and transition logic to maintain separation between blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming with separation constraints between selected intervals
- Efficient computation of range maxima for subarray value calculation
- Backpointer implementation for reconstructing optimal block endpoints
- Time-space trade-off analysis: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space when using precomputed range maximum queries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Precompute range maxima
  const rangeMax = Array(n).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let currentMax = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      currentMax = Math.max(currentMax, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = currentMax;
    }
  }

  // Base case: 0 blocks
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  // Fill DP table
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i-1; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s-2 >= 0 ? s-2 : 0;
        if (dp[prevIdx][t-1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = rangeMax[s-1][i-1] + dp[prevIdx][t-1];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = s-1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], reconstruct: () => reconstructBlocks(prev, n, k) };
}
```

